  "M200_1e14": 0.0,
  "notes": "The Boötes Void (Kirshner et al. 1981, ApJ 248, L57) — one of the largest known cosmic voids, ~130 Mpc radius, centred at z≈0.058 (250 Mpc). Originally discovered as a ~33,000 km/s gap in the CfA survey with only a handful of galaxies; now NED-confirmed to contain ~2,836 catalog members mostly on the void wall. Interior density <5% of cosmic mean — the deepest known emptiness in the observable universe. Population dominated by blue star-forming irregulars and Sb spirals with elevated HI fractions, suppressed AGN activity, and low metallicities (Beygu et al. 2013, Beygu+Kreckel studies). The 25 named wall galaxies here (NGC 6272, NGC 5277, IC 4572, Mrk 284, etc.) were confirmed by NED TAP queries. CGCG 166-016 and CGCG 165-007 are the deepest confirmed residents (~65-77 Mpc from centre). No hot ICM (tx_kev=0); no ram-pressure stripping. Far-wall boundary approaches the Hercules supercluster (east) and the Perseus-Pisces filament (north). Exoloc addresses: exotopia:void-world:Bootes-Void/{galaxy-id}.",
